S(6,"w6t5","short",3,"Describe the main stages of the formation and development of Kazakh cinema in the Soviet period.",[["1929","vostokkino"],["1934","newsreel"],["amangeldy","1938"],["mosfilm","lenfilm","evacuat","war"],["kazakhfilm","1960"],["aimanov","begalin","khodzhikov","karsakbayev"],["ideology","identity"]],"Cinema began with the Vostokkino studio (1929) and the Alma-Ata newsreel studio (1934). 'Amangeldy' (1938) was the first feature film on a Kazakh theme (E. Umurzakov). In WWII Mosfilm and Lenfilm were evacuated to Almaty (1941–44), creating the Central United Film Studio and training local film-makers. In 1960 the studio became Kazakhfilm; the 1950s–70s were a 'golden age' with directors Aimanov (Our Dear Doctor), Begalin (Song of Manshuk), Khodzhikov (Kyz Zhibek), Karsakbayev (My Name is Kozha). Cinema promoted Soviet ideology but also developed Kazakh identity; in 1984 Kazakhfilm was named after Aimanov.");
