M(10,"w10t6","def",2,"Cultural ecology studies…",["how cultures adapt to their natural environment","the semiotics of urban architecture","memory institutions","digital humanities"],"Culture–environment relationship.");
M(10,"w10t6","def",2,"'Zhailau' refers to…",["the summer pasture","the winter camp","the autumn pasture","a type of yurt"],"Kystau = winter; koktau = spring; kuzeu = autumn.");
M(10,"w10t6","event",2,"Which modern environmental disaster in Kazakhstan is linked to Soviet irrigation?",["the drying of the Aral Sea","the Zheltoqsan events","the Mongol invasions","the Dzungar invasions"],"Aral Sea.");
S(10,"w10t6","short",2,"Explain the role of nature in Kazakh national identity using the concept of cultural ecology.",[["steward","adapt","environment"],["nomad","season","pasture","zhailau"],["tengri","zher-su","sacred"],["steppe","horse","eagle"],["aral","semipalatinsk","ecolog","taza"]],"Cultural ecology (Julian Steward) studies how culture adapts to the environment. Kazakh nomadism was an adaptation to the steppe: seasonal pastures (kystau, koktau, zhailau, kuzeu), four kinds of livestock, the portable yurt. Beliefs in Tengri and Zher-Su made land and water sacred. Nature (steppe, horse, eagle) appears in symbols and identity. Modern challenges – the Aral Sea, the Semipalatinsk test site – and responses like Taza Kazakhstan show the link between environment and culture today.");
M(10,"w10t7","people",2,"The concept of 'collective memory' (1925) belongs to…",["Maurice Halbwachs","Pierre Nora","Jan Assmann","Julian Steward"],"Halbwachs.");
M(10,"w10t7","people",2,"'Lieux de mémoire' (sites of memory) is a concept of…",["Pierre Nora","Maurice Halbwachs","Aleida Assmann","Roland Barthes"],"Nora.");
M(10,"w10t7","compare",3,"According to the Assmanns, everyday oral memory spanning about 80–100 years is…",["communicative memory","cultural memory","collective unconscious","digital memory"],"Cultural memory = long-term, institutional.");
S(10,"w10t7","short",2,"How do institutions preserve and transmit cultural memory between generations? Give Kazakh examples.",[["museum"],["school","universit","education"],["librar","archive"],["preserv","collect","store"],["transmit","generation","teach"],["alzhir","national museum","e-museum","national library","tugan zher","archive"]],"Institutions collect, preserve, interpret and transmit memory: museums (National Museum in Astana, ALZhIR memorial, e-Museum) exhibit objects and narratives; schools and universities teach language, history and literature; libraries and archives (National Academic Library, Central State Archive, Archive-2025) keep documents; cultural organizations (opera, Kazakhfilm) perform heritage; local projects (Tugan Zher) keep village memory. They turn short communicative memory into long-term cultural memory (Assmann).");

